86045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438045}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "95fb5bb7-a16c-591d-9bc5-3cc80a4f1c75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438044 :task-item/_responses [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186574008 :task-item/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111133} :task-item/status {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045571}}]} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7b0dbf4-aa8c-5f41-bd3d-257a796c46ed" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439051}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "71c3d793-6b55-5392-ac94-1149b022497f" :question/math-academy-id "q-287966" :question/problem "Which of the following equations has infinitely many solutions?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $2x=x+1$\n- **II.** $2x+1=2x+1$\n- **III.** $2x+1=2x+3$" :question/worked-solution "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & =x+1 \\\\ 2x-x & =x+1-x \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $2x=x+1$ has the unique solution $x=1.$\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+1 & =2x+1 \\\\ 2x+1-2x & =2x+1-2x \\\\ 1 & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $x.$ Therefore, the equation $2x+1=2x+1$ has infinitely many solutions.\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+1 & =2x+3 \\\\ 2x+1-2x & =2x+3-2x \\\\ 1 & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is false for all values of $x.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :task-item/_content [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186574008 :task-item/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111133} :task-item/elapsed-seconds 18.822 :task-item/id #uuid "1f9b14d4-6e2f-4f34-b5da-be63eb4b5b23" :task-item/next {:db/id #edb/ref 1759218