choices [{:answer/id #uuid "82de10d3-76ce-5fc3-b275-3168f31a74f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477351} {:answer/id #uuid "d31e4384-7b72-540a-9f5d-372195b98996" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477352}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "82de10d3-76ce-5fc3-b275-3168f31a74f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477351} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8a46a7b-599a-5c9b-bddd-9358f4dbbba0" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477645}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "599179be-ddc2-50ab-a431-64168ebbcf12" :question/math-academy-id "q-53000" :question/problem "![](images/6b/6bffbc53c9e35da03655638ca40c5899bc5290cbadf3d14aef8e82585f14e004.png)\n\nWhich of the following terms describe the shape above?\n\nI. Kite\n\nII. Rectangle\n\nIII. Trapezoid\n\nUse the convention that a kite has at least two pairs of equal adjacent sides and a trapezoid has exactly one pair of parallel sides.\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "First, recall the hierarchy diagram of quadrilaterals, where each shape below another is a specific type of the one above it.\n\n![](images/1d/1dbe4c3e8ea97c0ac077c37143802366c5817d86965ab1dbc5abf54cd01d27c3.png)\n\nLet's now look at the terms one by one.\n\n- The given figure is a quadrilateral with two pairs of congruent sides that are adjacent to each other, so it is a kite.\n\n- Since the four angles of the quadrilateral are right angles, it is a rectangle.\n\n- The quadrilateral has two pairs of opposite sides that are parallel to each other, not one pair. So it is **not** a trapezoid.\n\nConsequently, the given shape is described by terms I (kite), and II (rectangle)."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111262 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110778 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "34ed7bcd-3c06-592e-906c-5010e80370db" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Quadrilaterals" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049068 :topic/id #uuid "d78ca6bb-945f-51db-a4af-fc777024df22" :topic/math-academy-id 1459 :topic/title "Classifying Quadrilaterals"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9e8af694-f9ab-5243-92cd-ee16bcb00f53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477353} {:answer/id #uuid "b3f82d28-0dda-5a10-a974-9a87aef74dd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477354}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b3f82d28-0dda-5a10-a974-9a87aef74dd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477354} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8ffdb2a-36e3-5f56-bd99-527b102cbbbc" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477646} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f111530a-98a7-5bd5-ae40-2760bcbe73c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477355} {:answer/id #uuid "fe6a353f-0be7-552a-b591-45c60421393f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477356}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fe6a353f-0be7-552a-b591-45c60421393f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477356} :answer-field/id #uuid "babaeecd-6a33-5cff-812a-06f3b54955a1" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477647} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22ec773a-7124-539e-ae98-4f4dcca6ddff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477357} {:answer/id #uuid "2e9b4b1c-1ba6-5907-baad-e342f1982ac0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477358}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "22ec773a-7124-539e-ae98-4f4dcca6ddff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477357} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff4d475d-6602-5dcb-b458-27e16262b216" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477648}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5c9c084b-0547-549b-b2ae-ba90597387ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-53010" :question/problem "![](images/b6/b68f8d3d0634999697051b668c06dd34c1191905a7bebda79925cd437d454d36.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the trapezoid above?\n\nI. $\\overset{―}{AB}∥\\overset{―}{CD}.$\n\nII. $m∠ABC={90}^{∘}.$\n\nIII. $\\overset{―}{AD}∥\\overset{―}{BC}.$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The trapezoid has a pair of parallel sides, $\\overset{―}{AB}∥\\overset{―}{CD}.$\n\n- Statement II is true. We can see that $∠ABC$ is a right angle, that is, $m∠ABC={90}^{∘}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. The trapezoid has one pair of parallel sides, $\\overset{―}{AB}∥\\overset{―}{CD}.$ We can see that $\\overset{―}{AD}$ and $\\overset{―}{BC}$ are not parallel.\n\nIn conclusion, statements I and II are true."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111356 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110571 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "67692569-e3a4-51e8-99ac-79d8b3081363" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Points that Lie on the Curve of the Inverse Sine Function" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050064 :topic/id #uuid "2a546664-10ba-55b4-ae58-ccb5dd16dca0" :topic/math-academy-id 1483 :topic/title "Graphing the Inverse Sine Function"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e99a9025-fb5a-54a5-9ab7-72135a77225a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477371} {:answer/id #uuid "31d95762-28dc-50a8-9be3-b41da2513393" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477372}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e99a9025-fb5a-54a5-9ab7-72135a77225a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477371} :answer-field/id #uuid "ebe38af3-d0d5-5d75-8290-3d1c253f0e72" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477655} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a190686-8589-5b4f-bc1a-09311cd52f78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477373} {:answer/id #uuid "ab355908-fb0d-548d-91e1-477450397db4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477374}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ab355908-fb0d-548d-91e1-477450397db4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477374} :answer-field/id #uuid "020c5de8-ab36-5db9-93c8-08f5b80d9611" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477656} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05fb94e2-3269-55ad-b4e5-f2eae77367f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477375} {:answer/id #uuid "e76f3778-f579-5d7f-8eb5-1903f27e06d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477376}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "05fb94e2-3269-55ad-b4e5-f2eae77367f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477375} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c0ede3e-962d-534c-8407-ad55a5311589" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477657}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "183017f0-e71f-5632-89e1-4603485e8f26" :question/math-academy-id "q-65744" :question/problem "Which of the following points lies on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x?$\n\nI. $(\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{1}{2})$\nII. $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{3})$\nIII. $(0,2\\pi )$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "In order for a point $(a,b)$ to lie on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x,$ the following conditions must hold:\n\n- The reflected point $(b,a)$ must lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ meaning $a=\\operatorname{sin}⁡b.$\n\n- The $y$-value of the point must lie in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x,$ which is $y\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of the points. \n\n- First, we consider the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{1}{2}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{6})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$  $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{1}{2})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x.$\n\n- Next, we consider the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{3}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is $y\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}],$ and $y=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{3})$ does lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x.$\n\n- Finally, we consider the point $(0,2\\pi ).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(2\\pi ,0)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is $y\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}],$ and $y=2\\pi $ does **not** lie within this range. $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(0,2\\pi )$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111361 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110575 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "af0b18ca-54a2-5a24-a670-faa311096abc" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Points that Lie on the Curve of the Inverse Cosine Function" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048794 :topic/id #uuid "bf6b1d4a-a2a5-5f59-9697-5ed210454e3e" :topic/math-academy-id 1486 :topic/title "Graphing the Inverse Cosine Function"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56708daa-2130-56c2-b5e5-0e10fce06ad0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477377} {:answer/id #uuid "069f7725-2ffb-56bc-b017-8a08b698c7d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477378}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "56708daa-2130-56c2-b5e5-0e10fce06ad0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477377} :answer-field/id #uuid "26418a45-1f09-5776-8b0c-9a26dcd446be" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477658} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6da7d1b6-c1c9-5c43-b65c-bb52d0bfe2e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477379} {:answer/id #uuid "6afe67fd-e587-5ac9-aa45-2ce8c2ddabaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477380}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6da7d1b6-c1c9-5c43-b65c-bb52d0bfe2e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477379} :answer-field/id #uuid "c101de9b-6361-5a36-80c8-a332d15ffc8e" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477659} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6fa74c9b-7103-53bd-92a6-59bf19bfd993" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477381} {:answer/id #uuid "4e8b820f-4706-5ad1-a108-f9996746f3da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477382}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4e8b820f-4706-5ad1-a108-f9996746f3da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477382} :answer-field/id #uuid "aaa43211-a26e-56b1-b5fe-f62fbbce7f90" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477660}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4c5ae160-d78c-57fc-8666-ed9ed6778702" :question/math-academy-id "q-65828" :question/problem "Which of the following points lies on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x?$\n\nI. $(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{1}{2})$\nII. $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{7\\pi }{4})$\nIII. $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{4})$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "In order for a point $(a,b)$ to lie on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x,$ the following conditions must hold:\n\n- The reflected point $(b,a)$ must lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$\n\n- The $y$-value of the point must lie in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x,$ which is $y\\in [0,\\pi ].$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of the points. \n\n- First, we consider the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{1}{2}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{3})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$  $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{1}{2})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x.$\n\n- Next, we consider the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{7\\pi }{4}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{7\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x$ is $y\\in [0,\\pi ],$ and $y=\\frac{7\\pi }{4}$ does **not** lie within this range.   $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{7\\pi }{4})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x.$\n\n- Finally, we consider the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{4}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{3\\pi }{4},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x$ is $y\\in [0,\\pi ],$ and $y=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{4})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111362 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110588 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f045b43-4785-5989-8b5a-dcb8401d1bb3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Points that Lie on the Graph of the Inverse Tangent Function" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049656 :topic/id #uuid "08bc988d-9fd4-51b9-b9a1-01573e3679f4" :topic/math-academy-id 1487 :topic/title "Graphing the Inverse Tangent Function"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08aa76b4-af00-5cf5-86f0-d9111d90209b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460137} {:answer/id #uuid "48152d24-ed22-5738-bb6f-a224e33b9cb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460138}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "08aa76b4-af00-5cf5-86f0-d9111d90209b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460137} :answer-field/id #uuid "ceee4737-1a31-555e-a291-33a7162e80e9" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460935} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e2def92a-e98b-544b-8a7e-81a30a765f6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460139} {:answer/id #uuid "3cae6124-4500-57bb-8c62-a8be4a3fe484" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460140}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3cae6124-4500-57bb-8c62-a8be4a3fe484" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460140} :answer-field/id #uuid "d9ad9f45-e5cf-50e8-b7c8-fa2cf2043205" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460936} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "63bbc76f-c0c6-5b17-a51d-501f38311609" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460141} {:answer/id #uuid "76297125-be34-545f-a9ff-ebed6caa4894" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460142}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "63bbc76f-c0c6-5b17-a51d-501f38311609" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460141} :answer-field/id #uuid "749729d4-1969-57b1-9375-a3cfffb6e59e" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460937}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "00b3cd73-63d0-506c-b47a-e48c2b7ba801" :question/math-academy-id "q-65830" :question/problem "Which of the following points lies on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x?$\n- **I.** $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},1)$\n- **II.** $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4})$\n- **III.** $(\\sqrt{3},\\frac{4\\pi }{3})$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "In order for a point $(a,b)$ to lie on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x,$ the following conditions must hold:\n\n- The reflected point $(b,a)$ must lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$\n\n- The $y$-value of the point must lie in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x,$ which is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of the points. \n\n- First, we consider the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},1).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$  $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},1)$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$\n\n- Next, we consider the point $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},1)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ and $y=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ does lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$\n\n- Finally, we consider the point $(\\sqrt{3},\\frac{4\\pi }{3}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{4\\pi }{3},\\sqrt{3})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ and $y=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$ does **not** lie within this range. $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\sqrt{3},\\frac{4\\pi }{3})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111365 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110575 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "af0b18ca-54a2-5a24-a670-faa311096abc" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Points that Lie on the Curve of the Inverse Cosine Function" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048794 :topic/id #uuid "bf6b1d4a-a2a5-5f59-9697-5ed210454e3e" :topic/math-academy-id 1486 :topic/title "Graphing the Inverse Cosine Function"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d39661f-79dd-50fe-a330-60dbe1bceca7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477383} {:answer/id #uuid "8493235e-4c7c-5136-a95e-233e93def0f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477384}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1d39661f-79dd-50