7592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447788} :answer-field/id #uuid "97ac8052-e451-5424-ac15-12e5bcf4b7f8" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448934}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a98e5d99-323b-544b-bce7-36fa3d3ba9e1" :question/math-academy-id "q-106806" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the set of all $2\\times 2$ real matrices with determinant $1.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** For any $A\\in S,$ we have $1\\cdot A=A.$\n\n- **II.** For any $A\\in S$ and $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we have $(\\alpha +\\beta )A=\\alpha A+\\beta A.$\n\n- **III.** There is a matrix $A\\in S$ such that $\\frac{1}{2}(2A)\\ne A.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any $2\\times 2$ matrix $A=({a}_{ij})\\in S,$ the entries are just real numbers. Thus, we have\n\n$$\n1\\cdot {a}_{ij}={a}_{ij},\n$$\n\nand hence,\n\n$$\n1\\cdot A=A.\n$$\n\n- Statements II is true. Indeed, for any $2\\times 2$ matrix $A=({a}_{ij})\\in S,$ the entries are just real numbers. Thus, for any $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n(\\alpha +\\beta ){a}_{ij}=\\alpha {a}_{ij}+\\beta {a}_{ij},\n$$\n\nand hence,\n\n$$\n(\\alpha +\\beta )A=\\alpha A+\\beta A.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. For a matrix in $A\\in S,$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}(2A)=(\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2)A=1\\cdot A=A\n$$\n\nsince the scalar multiplication of matrices is associative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124770 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124615 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f4f2f4f4-af4a-597d-b6c6-afb82e708fc2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Steady-State Vectors of Stochastic Matrices" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048393 :topic/id #uuid "9babae1d-ca0d-5438-a2e4-a65dbba9f7ef" :topic/math-academy-id 1913 :topic/title "Steady-State Vectors"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e87c8124-0ff2-547b-8e5b-1dd24c33ff63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459395} {:answer/id #uuid "1db6c273-8e01-545f-91b6-8ebdebf4da20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459396}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1db6c273-8e01-545f-91b6-8ebdebf4da20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459396} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff666d64-9efb-5fa3-a856-3094022b00fa" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460564} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3df9a2b8-8bba-539f-90aa-2cc738bd7714" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459397} {:answer/id #uuid "4ac0ac9d-8b8c-5d0a-9d3f-28652b7eb407" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459398}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4ac0ac9d-8b8c-5d0a-9d3f-28652b7eb407" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459398} :answer-field/id #uuid "c610aaa1-fb31-5f5b-906e-c90bf52d5364" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460565} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "449c8856-7e56-53ff-9d15-616c80d5a4ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459399} {:answer/id #uuid "029706cb-7965-5a82-8339-455757b093e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459400}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "029706cb-7965-5a82-8339-455757b093e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459400} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba85054f-f2a2-5c15-b38d-4e5781404347" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460566}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3662430d-84f0-5632-b066-9a7b273fafeb" :question/math-academy-id "q-110189" :question/problem "$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0.2 & 0.7 \\\\ 0.8 & 0.3\\end{bmatrix},\\,q=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{7}{15} \\\\ \\frac{8}{15}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the stochastic matrix $P$ and vector $q$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** $q$ is a stochastic vector\n- **II.** $Pq=q$\n- **III.** $q$ is a steady-state vector of $P$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Recall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that $Pq=q.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $q$ is a stochastic vector since its entries are non-negative and add up to $1.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have \n\n$$\nPq=\\begin{bmatrix}0.2 & 0.7 \\\\ 0.8 & 0.3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{7}{15} \\\\ \\frac{8}{15}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1.4}{15}+\\frac{5.6}{15} \\\\ \\frac{5.6}{15}+\\frac{2.4}{15}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{7}{15} \\\\ \\frac{8}{15}\\end{bmatrix}=q.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $q$ is a stochastic vector such that $Pq=q.$ So, it's a steady-state vector of $P.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124771 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124615 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f4f2f4f4-af4a-597d-b6c6-afb82e708fc2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Steady-State Vectors of Stochastic Matrices" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048393 :topic/id #uuid "9babae1d-ca0d-5438-a2e4-a65dbba9f7ef" :topic/math-academy-id 1913 :topic/title "Steady-State Vectors"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c39d6007-3d64-5685-b8c2-3bdda3ca55e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459401} {:answer/id #uuid "13c1dc0d-cdb6-5c69-8cf6-39d5bc66cef4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459402}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "13c1dc0d-cdb6-5c69-8cf6-39d5bc66cef4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459402} :answer-field/id #uuid "959b7ae6-a403-5443-8e59-429047b0a0f0" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460567} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a223a2e-adf2-5b55-8492-76af71dae7f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459403} {:answer/id #uuid "5c89a81b-e4dc-5955-8a3f-a64c32b52cc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459404}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1a223a2e-adf2-5b55-8492-76af71dae7f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459403} :answer-field/id #uuid "c5f2897a-a72c-566d-a233-47a13942ed65" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460568} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b0493696-0e98-587f-9bf7-c908798a7c68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459405} {:answer/id #uuid "e539bf2b-6167-5a95-961d-e33b0f253ec1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459406}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b0493696-0e98-587f-9bf7-c908798a7c68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459405} :answer-field/id #uuid "2cd17c31-ac61-58e8-b271-80f4eeae1164" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460569}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5d2ea1fa-42c3-5215-bede-8b745015a967" :question/math-academy-id "q-110239" :question/problem "$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0.5 \\\\ 1 & 0.5\\end{bmatrix},\\,q=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\frac{3}{4}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the stochastic matrix $P$ and vector $q$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** $q$ is a stochastic vector\n- **II.** $Pq=q$\n- **III.** $q$ is a steady-state vector of $P$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Recall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that $Pq=q.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $q$ is a stochastic vector since its entries are non-negative and add up to $1.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Note that\n\n$$\nPq=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0.5 \\\\ 1 & 0.5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\frac{3}{4}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0+\\frac{1.5}{4} \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1.5}{4}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{8} \\\\ \\frac{5}{8}\\end{bmatrix}\\ne q.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $Pq\\ne q,$ it is not a steady-state vector of $P.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124795 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124635 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b34c9c06-e884-5758-a184-39330a07d8cd" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Interior and Boundary Points of Sets of Real Numbers" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048942 :topic/id #uuid "cdb710e4-10cf-5554-8041-f24c448bbdae" :topic/math-academy-id 1930 :topic/title "Interior and Boundary Points"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a3a1f96d-9a3f-5c1c-ab2d-e8d5123ffa2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459569} {:answer/id #uuid "f84f80cc-e9aa-54c7-90dd-385e387a4d7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459570}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a3a1f96d-9a3f-5c1c-ab2d-e8d5123ffa2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459569} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b30ceda-f821-5ff6-ad47-743f125d45a9" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460651} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b4febe7f-c323-5336-a0c7-06d8d2c80d04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459571} {:answer/id #uuid "9f13e4db-10a8-5be2-b31f-32fb5ffee6bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459572}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9f13e4db-10a8-5be2-b31f-32fb5ffee6bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459572} :answer-field/id #uuid "28dc75a2-4163-519b-a815-660274285a03" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460652} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ad1a624-9364-5075-a11b-32a164535568" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459573} {:answer/id #uuid "bf8a8f78-6603-53fe-bea5-f58fe4983bd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459574}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bf8a8f78-6603-53fe-bea5-f58fe4983bd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459574} :answer-field/id #uuid "da7ab65c-c9c2-5efe-9a3a-405cb3117a7d" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460653}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "98524e7d-f97e-5125-b634-0488b239406e" :question/math-academy-id "q-156152" :question/problem "![](images/96/9685260f754e0d6333de6f24ffbf3908c055d6e4fe2d3ffe47391410735c7df2.png)\n\nConsider the set $M=[-2,1)$ and the points $A,$ $B,$ and $C,$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ is an interior point of the set $M$\n- $B$ is an interior point of the set $M$\n- $C$ is a boundary point of the set $M$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}\\in R$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is *smaller than* $\\delta :$ \n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nA neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is given by the open interval $({x}_{0}-\\delta ,{x}_{0}+\\delta ),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/f8/f889352f25e7936d0e5ea76585a979efbb301da61f7e99a937e979af93ee0664.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, $A$ is not an interior point of $M$ since any neighborhood of $A$ contains at least one point *outside* the set.\n\n![](images/39/39f7097f82f19d703208f9e4e8638e4d64aa417ad9cf4bc1f90092eb0c5e5639.png)\n\n- Statement II is true. The point $B$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $B$ that is fully contained inside the set $M.$\n\n![](images/3f/3ffa79d3052247aecd276c7794a498340acb66a1e74aeddcdd7800b1e9704506.png)\n\n- Statement III is true. The point $C$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $C$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$\n\n![](images/f1/f116e950d99e0a00908c6c40a3c788cc450efa120843369e13f857cb084d8a1e.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'II and III only.'"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124809 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124635 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b34c9c06-e884-5758-a184-39330a07d8cd" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Interior and Boundary Points of Sets of Real Numbers" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048942 :topic/id #uuid "cdb710e4-10cf-5554-8041-f24c448bbdae" :topic/math-academy-id 1930 :topic/title "Interior and Boundary Points"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39c54d92-9a63-547e-83a6-09e42ce30323" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459581} {:answer/id #uuid "4ddd2812-c56d-5519-a39d-508d31a5428b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459582}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "39c54d92-9a63-547e-83a6-09e42ce30323" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459581} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf3dbdc3-cf27-553c-a74a-470a04811391" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460657} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f990409d-6ce2-5be1-8047-2dc346f081a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459583} {:answer/id #uuid "9b7e419d-fcc3-5d57-89e7-20d574c8c131" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459584}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f990409d-6ce2-5be1-8047-2dc346f081a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459583} :answer-field/id #uuid "d12b3895-dc1d-5449-ab55-6077210053c7" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460658} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "602ffe5c-6faf-50ef-bb1a-e86513e0a517" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459585} {:answer/id #uuid "3233a795-9f20-57cb-9822-63e4d2cb480f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459586}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3233a795-9f20-57cb-9822-63e4d2cb480f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459586} :answer-field/id #uuid "e6b69363-0e22-5cfc-8e18-c0d379254f70" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460659}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b288f6b3-b69c-5d49-b36c-166c5ccca7a2" :question/math-academy-id "q-159266" :question/problem "![](images/51/51ccbc1cf370686432f144f9bf3f8f389b5ee1beb652b704266708fc1e9d8e6d.png)\n\nConsider the set $M=\\{0\\}\\cup (2,\\infty )$ and the points $A,$ $B,$ and $C,$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $A$ is an interior point of the set $M$\n\nII. $B$ is a boundary point of the set $M$\n\nIII. $C$ is an interior point of the set $M$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\nIf none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}\\in R$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is *smaller than* $\\delta :$ \n\n$$\n\\{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta \\}\n$$\n\nA neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is given by the open interval $({x}_{0}-\\delta ,{x}_{0}+\\delta ),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/f8/f889352f25e7936d0e5ea76585a979efbb301da61f7e99a937e979af93ee0664.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The point $A$ is *not* an interior point of $M$ since any neighborhood of $A$ contains at least one point *outside* the set.\n\n![](images/ba/baece8024a1c90a9d83efc2669ecbdda20867f0cebc31670b80deadd36526cf2.png)\n\n- Statement II is false. The point $B$ is *not* a boundary point since there exists any neighborhood of $B$ that is fully contained *outside* the set $M.$\n\n![](images/19/1943ab4d5fa1cc8358352e325e1dd44d48c45922dabf09813a8568266e466cf7.png)\n\n- Statement III is true. The point $C$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $C$ that is fully contained inside the set $M.$\n\n![](images/01/01c22233e2876de03185de67738b46116f70e9084d57ea6c0884fc483e920c81.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'III only.'"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124814 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124647 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f0fb118f-c1cc-51fd-a8cc-c29cf705acfe" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Local Extrema of a Multivariable Function" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049676 :topic/id #uuid "0a177390-6525-5d0c-8721-f7f75db39408" :topic/math-academy-id 1945 :topic/title "Global vs. Local Extrema and Critical Points of Multivariable Functions"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2bc876d5-f44a-5998-985c-5ffee4f76b67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477171} {:answer/id #uuid "77d03628-028e-52ab-8003-a110d2ad4fd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477172}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "77d03628-028e-52ab-8003-a110d2ad4fd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477172} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f1ebfba-def7-577f-907c-a0d42a0db87e" :answer-field/key "A" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477555} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "af28443d-0296-53d2-9067-66e70b53c0cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477173} {:answer/id #uuid "7040459f-fe8a-5b70-a4a7-46f669077ea2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477174}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "af28443d-0296-53d2-9067-66e70b53c0cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477173} :answer-field/id #uuid "4606d9f9-6b0e-5c78-88f5-bc456a4930fb" :answer-field/key "B" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477556} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "335c0cd8-081f-5da4-8331-b78c0f8bb7dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477175} {:answer/id #uuid "44288354-bf2e-5a0f-91e8-6bb62350cda3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477176}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "335c0cd8-081f-5da4-8331-b78c0f8bb7dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477175} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d95946f-7976-5aeb-8e94-5c9d5ea818ee" :answer-field/key "C" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477557} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b04474b9-64a1-5d86-b5a5-dc0aa5b99b7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477177} {:answer/id #uuid "20f27812-b4ae-5c70-86be-39936a5a8ad5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477178}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "20f27812-b4ae-5c70-86be-39936a5a8ad5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477178} :answer-field/id #uuid "6810b078-209d-5aba-bb8f-1806d12074a9" :answer-field/key "D" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477558}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0151a70d-ce12-5172-8abb-638426f2b102" :question/math-academy-id "q-162747" :question/problem "![](images/b6/b698fe735caaf3bc260c1e921bb95596df8869eba67ec9ce72484b4d86cc8c69.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above. Which of the points shown are local minimum points?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A function $f(x,y)$ has a local minimum point at $(a,b)$ if $f(x,y)\\ge f(a,b)$ for all points $(x,y)$ in a neighborhood of $(a,b).$ \n\nIn other words, we can take a neighborhood of $(x,y)$-values such that the point $(x,y,f(x,y))$ is the lowest point on the graph in the neighborhood.\n\nSuch regions exist for the points $A$ and $D.$ Hence $A$ and $D$ are local minimum points.\n\n**Note**: For the point $D({x}_{D},{y}_{D},{z}_{D}),$ there are points $(x,y)$ in the surrounding regions such that $f({x}_{D},{y}_{D})=f(x,y).$ However, since the definition of local extrema is non-strict, $f(x,y)\\ge f(a,b)$, the point $D$ does satisfy the conditions for a local minimum.\n\n![](images/0a/0a371aa559222bd28c37677bace18eb912bdc978e364bab4cb3d83f7a310de3f.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124821 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124570 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "40d58ee6-1121-5ddc-a96f-1a7fa7a193dd" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Local Extrema Using Sylvester's Criterion" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049676 :topic/id #uuid "0a177390-6525-5d0c-8721-f7f75db39408" :topic/math-academy-id 1945 :topic/title "Global vs. Local Extrema and Critical Points of Multivariable Functions"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f9ab465-9b60-5926-9caf-66fa38e4437c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448472} {:answer/id #uuid "41ff740e-9971-5305-8cca-6d16b6176274" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448473}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "41ff740e-9971-5305-8cca-6d16b6176274" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448473} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e509c73-9172-59d0-a89f-3107e3fd27d0" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449276} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "208f176c-8dfc-594d-b8d8-8273c02ccbfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448474} {:answer/id #uuid "a82bd6fd-6532-53f7-b93a-eee32c232323" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448475}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "208f176c-8dfc-594d-b8d8-8273c02ccbfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448474} :answer-field/id #uuid "06489517-8558-587b-98d1-a63c1fea14be" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449277} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e0cf8462-0245-553e-8f2f-32ebff1011e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448476} {:answer/id #uuid "34965ccb-0322-5f75-90dc-bd64d3a0a98f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448477}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e0cf8462-0245-553e-8f2f-32ebff1011e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448476} :answer-field/id #uuid "e666111a-27b4-5617-a072-b9aa01376d65" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449278}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b5496aa6-8f04-55cb-807e-38886a8c0e30" :question/math-academy-id "q-163722" :question/problem "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈-12+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}A(x-a),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${a}_{11}=-2$ and $det(A)=-24$\n\n- **II.** $A$ is positive-definite\n\n- **III.** $a$ is a local minimum of $f$" :question/worked-solution "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nIn this case, we have $A={f}^{″}(a).$ Now, before we examine the statements, let's recall how to categorize quadratic forms.\n\nLet ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=-2$ and\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 4\\end{aligned}|=-24.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=-2<0, \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 4\\end{aligned}|=-24<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the matrix $A$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the matrix $A$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, the critical point $a$ is a saddle point of $f.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :step/_content [{:activity/_steps {:activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049676 :topic/id #uuid "0a177390-6525-5d0c-8721-f7f75db39408" :topic/math-academy-id 1945 :topic/title "Global vs. Local Extrema and Critical Points of Multivariable Functions"} :activity/title "Global vs. Local Extrema and Critical Points of Multivariable Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519988} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520563}]} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124822 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124570 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "40d58ee6-1121-5ddc-a96f-1a7fa7a193dd" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Local Extrema Using Sylvester's Criterion" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049676 :topic/id #uuid "0a177390-6525-5d0c-8721-f7f75db39408" :topic/math-academy-id 1945 :topic/title "Global vs. Local Extrema and Critical Points of Multivariable Functions"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "095fbc4a-1777-5a55-a970-420ada306ee6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448478} {:answer/id #uuid "9a99bace-ff30-5ed5-a0e7-4ced50a5bbb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448479}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a99bace-ff30-5ed5-a0e7-4ced50a5bbb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448479} :answer-field/id #uuid "4017ed68-fcbb-59db-9e5f-e1de08b2d73c" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449279} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "394fa51a-598d-52f3-b478-46d352cda2a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448480} {:answer/id #uuid "c8e8a81d-7472-5c4e-a312-336416f4ec2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448481}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c8e8a81d-7472-5c4e-a312-336416f4ec2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448481} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d74ca00-9446-532b-9773-a227a0971587" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449280} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23795227-e3c9-5931-b521-8ee6756b3090" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448482} {:answer/id #uuid "f2439755-795b-54c3-ab71-3adc7e5c18ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448483}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f2439755-795b-54c3-ab71-3adc7e5c18ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448483} :answer-field/id #uuid "766f6f44-2888-5026-89cd-943039d2c099" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449281}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f4f668d6-a868-59b1-9e4d-3e52d3a52a67" :question/math-academy-id "q-163723" :question/problem "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{3}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈-9+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}A(x-a),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${a}_{11}=6$ and $det(A)>0$\n- **II.** $A$ is positive-definite\n- **III.** $a$ is a local minimum of $f$" :question/worked-solution "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nIn this case, we have $A={f}^{″}(a).$ Now, before we examine the statements, we recall how to categorize quadratic forms.\n\nLet ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=6$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2\\cdot 20 \\\\  & =40>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=6>0, \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}|=20>0 \\\\ {Δ}_{3} & =|A|=40>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the matrix $A$ is positive-definite.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since the matrix $A$ is positive-definite, the critical point $a$ is a local minimum of $f.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" :step/_content [{:activity/_steps {:activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049676 :topic/id #uuid "0a177390-6525-5d0c-8721-f7f75db39408" :topic/math-academy-id 1945 :topic/title "Global vs. Local Extrema and Critical Points of Multivariable Functions"} :activity/title "Global vs. Local Extrema and Critical Points of Multivariable Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556413} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557345}]} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124858 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124677 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e7d1368-06ee-5135-b9f4-d3d4d2756b32" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Regular and Irregular Singular Points" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049703 :topic/id #uuid "0cc17781-65fe-5316-8a26-4416142da248" :topic/math-academy-id 1880 :topic/title "Regular Singular Points"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d72d178f-ef2f-5eed-805a-5c8d7cd4466c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477229} {:answer/id #uuid "8f7434b3-d198-5065-9c07-b2008d79bf37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477230}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d72d178f-ef2f-5eed-8