db/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8fc9b8ec-a9a8-566c-b5b3-f80f696a5223" :question/math-academy-id "q-341230" :question/problem "$$\n(x-1){y}^{″}+{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{(x-1)^{2}}y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** The functions $P(x)=\\frac{1}{x-1}$ and $Q(x)=\\frac{1}{(x-1)^{3}}$ are analytic about $x=1.$\n- **II.** The functions $(x-1)P(x)$ and $(x-1)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=1.$\n- **III.** The point $x=1$ is an **irregular** singular point of the differential equation.\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "A singular point $a$ of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x)\n$$\n\nis regular if both $(x-a)P(x)$ and $(x-a)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, the singular point is irregular.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by $(x-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1){y}^{″}+{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{(x-1)^{2}}y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{x-1}{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{(x-1)^{3}}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{1}{x-1}\\,\\text{and}\\,Q(x)=\\frac{1}{(x-1)^{3}}.\n$$\n\nLet's now check each statement in turn.\n\n- Since the denominators of both $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ vanish at $x=1,$ they are *not* analytic about $x=1.$ So, statement I is false.\n\n- The function\n\n$$\n(x-1)P(x)=(x-1)\\frac{1}{x-1}=1\n$$\n\nis analytic about $x=1,$ while the function\n\n$$\n(x-1)^{2}Q(x)=(x-1)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{(x-1)^{3}}=\\frac{1}{x-1}\n$$\n\nis *not* analytic about $x=1$ since its denominator vanishes at $x=1.$ Therefore, statement II is false.\n\n- Since statement I is false, $x=1$ is a singular point. Also, since statement II is false, it follows that $x=1$ is an **irregular** singular point. Thus, statement III is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124862 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124490 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca956d3e-37a8-5f0f-a062-97d0567d54e6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Bases of Abstract Vector Spaces" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048552 :topic/id #uuid "a92f5097-81a3-51a2-90e4-397763d967f0" :topic/math-academy-id 1909 :topic/title "Bases in Abstract Vector Spaces"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fd54348e-839f-5cae-9afe-99ed67cf8c07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459817} {:answer/id #uuid "874d00d1-4fc0-5469-a008-54e2efa4cd11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459818}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fd54348e-839f-5cae-9afe-99ed67cf8c07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459817} :answer-field/id #uuid "b346d2e4-0441-5908-8b1c-74a0b502db91" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460775} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a7d484d5-f663-5bbd-9095-7d40da8bb576" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459819} {:answer/id #uuid "c75ef81f-4f9b-5101-9a16-5a70fe48a075" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459820}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a7d484d5-f663-5bbd-9095-7d40da8bb576" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459819} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d6e79c9-dcbd-5ff6-9c30-85b726114d52" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460776} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "123e34b6-3821-5e94-b0f8-f13e61739489" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459821} {:answer/id #uuid "8f3b6ce8-c555-5ed3-bb2a-7ee3e15ca1cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459822}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f3b6ce8-c555-5ed3-bb2a-7ee3e15ca1cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459822} :answer-field/id #uuid "b3d94169-1efc-5ce2-916e-c84630954a94" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460777}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6d5104d0-4e4f-5680-8c4e-7e5a0379f267" :question/math-academy-id "q-34638" :question/problem "Let $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ and $C=\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}\\}$ be bases of ${R}^{2}$ and ${R}^{3},$ respectively. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},x\\}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$\n\nII. $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ is a basis of ${R}^{3}$\n\nIII. $\\{{c}_{3},{c}_{2},{c}_{1}\\}$ is a basis of ${R}^{3}$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Since $B$ and $C$ are bases, the following properties must hold:\n\n- $B$ and $C$ are maximally linearly independent sets in ${R}^{2}$ and ${R}^{3},$ respectively, and\n\n- $B$ spans ${R}^{2},$ while $C$ spans ${R}^{3}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$, any vector of ${R}^{2},$ including the given vector $x,$ can be written as a linear combination of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}.$ This means that $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},x\\}$ is linearly dependent and therefore, cannot be a basis.\n\n- Statement II is false. The set $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ is not a basis of ${R}^{3}$ because the set contains vectors from another vector space.\n\n- Statement III is true. The set $\\{{c}_{3},{c}_{2},{c}_{1}\\}$ is a basis of ${R}^{3}$ because, by reordering the vectors, we obtain the basis $C.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124863 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124511 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "084a0a83-5ce6-5184-beb7-8ae7b4f1a799" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049340 :topic/id #uuid "ecf075bf-6d18-5a77-93a9-57ef73f45115" :topic/math-academy-id 1869 :topic/title "The Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Dimension, Rank and Nullity"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "235a52da-656a-5cf2-a401-0f42a3642391" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459823} {:answer/id #uuid "04790f9e-17da-5ed1-82d4-a7391bccbd0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459824}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "235a52da-656a-5cf2-a401-0f42a3642391" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459823} :answer-field/id #uuid "419ff1b3-8e47-5988-b4a7-49adadda7718" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460778} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e39055a2-7be3-5968-8135-398e60211c8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459825} {:answer/id #uuid "f28e5027-719a-5804-a84a-81f68d441b43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459826}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f28e5027-719a-5804-a84a-81f68d441b43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459826} :answer-field/id #uuid "c6ef0960-7c5f-543c-a81b-bf7733be9b0f" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460779} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3932a553-bb8b-5851-b93b-ecabd828b7b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459827} {:answer/id #uuid "909e13f6-b658-585b-af87-0fb072eb5e4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459828}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3932a553-bb8b-5851-b93b-ecabd828b7b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459827} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c740ae4-9a04-5de5-a467-c4e121394796" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460780}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a5b63a26-4713-5ecd-9d64-befaccc0bd77" :question/math-academy-id "q-34834" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix and \n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ 3{x}_{1} \\\\ 0 \\\\ {x}_{1} \\\\ -2{x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}\\,|\\,{x}_{1}\\in R\\}?\n$$\n\nI. $\\text{dim(Null}(A))=0$\n\nII. $A$ is not invertible\n\nIII. $\\text{rank}(A)=5$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\text{Col}(A) &={R}^{5} \\\\ \\text{dim(Col}(A)) &=5=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\ \\text{Null}(A) &=\\{0\\} \\\\ \\text{dim(Null}(A)) &=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\\right.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since \n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)=\\{{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\,|\\,{x}_{1}\\in R\\}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that \n\n$$\n\\text{dim(Null}(A))=1\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $\\text{dim(Null}(A))\\ne 0,$ then according to the invertible matrix theorem, the matrix $A$ can't be invertible.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $A$ is not invertible, then by the invertible matrix theorem, we must have $\\text{rank}(A)\\ne 5.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :step/_content [{:activity/_steps {:activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049340 :topic/id #uuid "ecf075bf-6d18-5a77-93a9-57ef73f45115" :topic/math-academy-id 1869 :topic/title "The Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Dimension, Rank and Nullity"} :activity/title "The Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Dimension, Rank and Nullity" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435669} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435679}]} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124868 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124509 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f020b27f-1cd4-550b-a83a-2afbb7e5db72" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Dimension of the Null Space of Matrices Using Inspection" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050996 :topic/id #uuid "7821b684-8118-5d42-9459-ebb20010e0ff" :topic/math-academy-id 1868 :topic/title "The Dimension of the Null Space of a Matrix"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3fc909b1-8785-5945-9459-d053d2c7079c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459835} {:answer/id #uuid "52c418de-d88b-5235-9bf5-be2556fb9846" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459836}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "52c418de-d88b-5235-9bf5-be2556fb9846" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459836} :answer-field/id #uuid "55564640-7655-5d3c-bf63-8297180991a2" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460784} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "169e939e-6301-511d-8c0c-090a2f18ccdd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459837} {:answer/id #uuid "9fd9e1cc-c00a-5419-a142-d9bc5d4f5345" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459838}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "169e939e-6301-511d-8c0c-090a2f18ccdd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459837} :answer-field/id #uuid "aca7871e-e659-5912-aa33-fc7d1986742f" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460785} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "97736f06-c485-5e15-91c2-2eba3c226429" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459839} {:answer/id #uuid "0670e564-4f2a-597b-bb60-ec48ef5ea2c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459840}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0670e564-4f2a-597b-bb60-ec48ef5ea2c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459840} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a13967f-4100-5e9e-a96a-a678a0efc380" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460786}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5f568aea-24ab-542a-9112-9bb117e4fc0c" :question/math-academy-id "q-34904" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** If $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$ then $\\text{dim(Null}(A))=0.$\n- **II.** If $B=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$ then $\\text{dim(Null}(B))=2.$\n- **III.** If $C=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$ then $\\text{dim(Null}(C))=1.$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "The dimension of the null space of a matrix is the number of non-pivot columns in the matrix. \n\nAlso, all of the given matrices are in row echelon form.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. The matrix $A$ has $2$ pivot columns and no non-pivot columns. So, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim(Null}(A))=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. The matrix $B$ has $2$ pivot column and $1$ non-pivot column (namely, the $3$rd column). So, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim(Null}(B))=1.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. The matrix $C$ has $2$ pivot columns and $1$ non-pivot column (namely, the $2$nd column). So, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim(Null}(C))=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" :step/_content [{:activity/_steps {:activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050996 :topic/id #uuid "7821b684-8118-5d42-9459-ebb20010e0ff" :topic/math-academy-id 1868 :topic/title "The Dimension of the Null Space of a Matrix"} :activity/title "The Dimension of the Null Space of a Matrix" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548348} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549075}]} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124880 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124478 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5f0d2e9-1cf6-5fe4-85cc-f401382f39b2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Non-Pivot Columns of a Matrix in Row Echelon Form" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049723 :topic/id #uuid "0e15397e-6c32-508c-b6ea-90f1a9e5a237" :topic/math-academy-id 1904 :topic/title "Identifying the Pivot Columns of a Matrix"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b787c6f6-5351-5e11-97e1-60d594c3bcbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186574349 :task-item/_responses [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186574063 :task-item/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124880} :task-item/status {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045571}}]} {:answer/id #uuid "65255442-2320-5c93-8e6a-cfb6ce46e3e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186574350}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b787c6f6-5351-5e11-97e1-60d594c3bcbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186574349 :task-item/_responses [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186574063 :task-item/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124880} :task-item/status {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045571}}]} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ed99ac0-e5b7-560b-99f7-88135ed6262b" :answer-field/key "Column 1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186574657} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "53f41b57-4f9f-516e-bd2c-2ecf22bc60cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186574351 :task-item/_responses [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186574063 :task-item/content {:db/i