d "d831ce98-574d-5301-837f-e31cf13ad32c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477345} {:answer/id #uuid "5f618cf6-eabe-599a-9b58-ed25914cd54a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477346}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5f618cf6-eabe-599a-9b58-ed25914cd54a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477346} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba078f9b-e67e-5ba0-9225-38240d09f6a1" :answer-field/key "IV" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477642}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1b6af7e1-2428-58bf-a924-9c6a5027415f" :question/math-academy-id "q-43774" :question/problem "Consider the vector space $C[0,1]$ of all continuous functions $f:[0,1]\\to R$ and its subset $D=\\{f\\in C[0,1]\\,|\\,f(0)=3\\}.$ \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $D$ is closed under function addition\n\nII. $D$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\nIII. $D$ is a subspace of $C[0,1]$\n\nIV. $D$ is **not** a subspace of $C[0,1]$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, if $f(x),g(x)\\in D,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f+g)(0) & =f(0)+g(0) \\\\  & =3+3 \\\\  & =6 \\\\  & \\ne 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $D$ is *not* closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, if $f(x)\\in D$ and $k=5\\in R,$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(kf)(0) & =(5f)(0) \\\\  & =5\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =15 \\\\  & \\ne 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $D$ is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is false, while statement IV is true. $D$ is *not* a subspace because it's not closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\"" :step/_content [{:activity/_steps {:activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050453 :topic/id #uuid "4a436386-bf41-55c7-b1d3-02adad8f3faf" :topic/math-academy-id 1907 :topic/title "Subspaces of Abstract Vector Spaces"} :activity/title "Subspaces of Abstract Vector Spaces" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186574947} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186575680}]} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125164 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124631 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "113a601e-220a-54a3-a64b-1e554bc3d79f" :knowledge-point/title "Сompleting a Sentence Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049371 :topic/id #uuid "ef6bb570-cff7-5ef3-a4ba-79063f5d81ad" :topic/math-academy-id 1962 :topic/title "The Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Linear Transformations"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "da97a065-c9ea-545d-9832-58ba5595c282" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460017} {:answer/id #uuid "44b485c0-4b9e-522d-95c5-7ceba53f1f39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460018}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "da97a065-c9ea-545d-9832-58ba5595c282" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460017} :answer-field/id #uuid "efff0b18-702b-56a0-8bdf-765f60a773b3" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460875} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "74ec198b-aef3-59b6-b091-30d662e9f4cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460019} {:answer/id #uuid "a7f8d74d-cce4-5aab-b18a-c61877075bdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460020}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a7f8d74d-cce4-5aab-b18a-c61877075bdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460020} :answer-field/id #uuid "9908404e-2262-51c4-b8db-2672114ba2d0" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460876} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a0734130-7b87-5408-bf8e-b097433b8280" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460021} {:answer/id #uuid "b49d1b8e-67c2-5af5-8d46-21f059350ab6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460022}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a0734130-7b87-5408-bf8e-b097433b8280" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460021} :answer-field/id #uuid "6141e94f-e3cc-500f-ba91-1f18aa4e01ca" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460877}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a059dc0b-5296-5e25-93e6-3ffd38125f6c" :question/math-academy-id "q-47556" :question/problem "Let $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T.$ Consider the following statement:\n\n*If $[1,-1]^{T}\\notin \\text{Im}(T)$, then $\\underline{\\phantom{xxxxxx}}.$*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\n- **I.** $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\}$\n\n- **II.** $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 2$\n\n- **III.** $T$ is invertible\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{2}$ into ${R}^{2},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{2} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=2 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$ does not lie in $\\text{Im}(T)$, then $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{2}.$ Therefore, according to the invertible matrix theorem, we must have $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne \\{0\\}.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{2}$, then $\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 2.$\n\n- Statement III is false. According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{2},$ the transformation $T$ can't be invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :step/_content [{:activity/_steps {:activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049371 :topic/id #uuid "ef6bb570-cff7-5ef3-a4ba-79063f5d81ad" :topic/math-academy-id 1962 :topic/title "The Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Linear Transformations"} :activity/title "The Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Linear Transformations" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417684} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417706}]}]
